{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments ["<b>Let the waters swarm with swarming creatures</b> Rashi writes (Rashi on Genesis 1:20): Every living thing that is not elevated above the earth is called שרץ — among birds, for example flies; among vermin, for example ants and worms; among creatures, for example the weasel and the mouse and the like, and all the fish. But what would the Rav say regarding the verse 'And you, be fruitful and multiply, swarm upon the earth and multiply in it' (Genesis 9:7), said of Noah and his sons? Likewise, 'which the waters swarmed' — according to this opinion it should say 'which swarmed in the waters.' And there are many birds that are not elevated above the earth, such as the height of the weasel, the mouse, and the bat, which has very small legs — why should it not be called 'swarming creature of the bird'? But the opinion of Onkelos (Onkelos on Genesis 1:20) is that the meaning of swarming (שריצה) is in the sense of movement. He renders 'the swarming creature and the creeping thing' (שרץ ורמש) as ריחשא דרחש. And this is well explained. And the swarming creatures are so called because their movement is constant. And it is possible that it is a compound expression: שרץ is called so because 'it runs' (שהוא רץ), and רמש because 'it creeps' upon the earth — it does not stand still and does not rest. And know that every bird that has four legs is called 'swarming creature of the bird' (Leviticus 11:20–23), because with its legs it supports itself and moves like the swarming creatures, and that which is not so is called 'winged bird,' because its primary movement is flight. And the meaning of 'and swarm upon the earth and be fruitful and multiply upon the earth' (Genesis 8:17) would be: that they walk throughout it and be fruitful and multiply upon it; 'swarm upon the earth and multiply in it' (Genesis 9:7) — move throughout it and multiply in it. And this is the reason for the repetition of 'and multiply' twice in the verse. If so, we interpret 'which the waters swarmed' (Genesis 1:21) as: which the waters moved and conveyed. Likewise, 'and the river shall swarm with frogs' (Exodus 7:28), and likewise, 'they were fruitful and swarmed' (Exodus 1:7) — they were fruitful and multiplied and moved in their multitude until the earth was filled with them. But Onkelos interpreted 'swarm upon the earth' (Genesis 9:7) as the concept of procreation: 'be born in the earth' (אתיילידו בארעא), for he treats the expression as borrowed from the swarming creatures — 'and you, be fruitful and multiply like the swarming creatures upon the earth and multiply in it.' Likewise, 'they were fruitful and swarmed' (Exodus 1:7) — their procreation was like that of the swarming creatures in abundance." "<b>And let fowl fly above the earth</b> On this day the creative utterance was in the waters, and on the sixth day it was on the earth. If so, the interpretation of 'and let fowl fly above the earth' is continuous: 'Let the waters swarm with swarming creatures, living soul, and fowl that shall fly.' And the verse that says 'and YHWH Elohim formed from the earth every beast of the field and every bird of the sky' (Genesis 2:19) is as if it said 'and YHWH Elohim formed every beast of the field from the earth, and He formed every bird of the sky' — and there are many like it. And so is the opinion of Rabbi Eliezer the Great in his chapters (beginning of chapter 9), who said: on the fifth day He brought forth from the waters every winged bird. But in the Gemara (Ḥullin 27b) they disagree about it: some say thus, that they were created from the waters, and some say that the bird was created from both of them — they said, from the mud they were created. And if so, because their origin was in the waters and the mud is the sea floor, for this reason the utterance concerning them was on the fifth day. Thus He said, 'Let the waters swarm with swarming creatures, living soul,' for the fish — their body and soul are from the waters, by the word of Elohim who brought them spirit from the elements, not so for man, whose body He separated from his soul, as it says, 'and YHWH Elohim formed the man, dust from the earth, and breathed into his nostrils the breath of life' (Genesis 2:7). And He did not mention 'soul' at all on the third day regarding vegetation, because the power of growth is a soul only in beings that possess movement alone. And according to the opinion of the Greeks, who say that just as growth in that which moves is only through soul, so too in vegetation it grows through soul, there would be a distinction: for this is 'living soul' — that is, a soul that has vitality in it — for there is a soul that has no vitality, and that is the soul of vegetation. And our Sages mentioned (Genesis Rabbah 41:1) regarding palm trees, that they have desire; but perhaps that is a power in vegetation and should not be called soul."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {0 "The Rav here refers to Maimonides, who follows Rashi's line of definition; Ramban challenges both.", 1 "The phrase 'the Greeks' (היונים) refers to Aristotelian philosophers; Ramban cites their position on plant souls to distinguish it from the Torah's usage."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-21T22:15:03.247861626Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}